Aesthetics and philosophy of art (@aesthetics-and-philosophy-of-art: 0, is #chapter, #outline) { r_28Z7XY50BG67Y8WWMVFRVWK0QW
Aesthetics investigates experience, form, beauty, expression, imagination, taste, and artistic value. This material asks how works acquire meaning, how interpretations and judgments are supported, and how artistic practices relate to their historical and social settings. Begin with particular works and contrasting readings, then develop the relevant philosophical arguments. Literature provides a continuing connection through narrative, poetic form, fictional worlds, emotional response, criticism, and the relationship between a work and its reader.

**Parent:** [Philosophy](../Philosophy.lingua#r_00000000000000000000001001).
